# Firmware Build Instructions for Windows

For the central Windows and Linux build guide, see [BUILD-README.md](BUILD-README.md).

This document describes how to install and verify the required FTDI drivers and how to build the firmware for 32-bit and 64-bit Windows.

## FTDI Driver

The firmware uses the FTDI D2XX driver.

Useful FTDI resources:

* Installation guides:
  https://ftdichip.com/document/installation-guides/

* D2XX drivers:
  https://ftdichip.com/drivers/d2xx-drivers/

* Windows driver package 2.12.36.20:
  https://ftdichip.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/CDM-v2.12.36.20-WHQL-Certified.zip

* Windows driver installer 2.12.36.20:
  https://ftdichip.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/CDM2123620_Setup.zip

## Verify the Installed FTDI Driver

Open **PowerShell** and run:

```powershell
pnputil /enum-drivers | Select-String -Pattern "FTDI" -Context 5,5
```

A correct installation of driver version `2.12.36.20` should show entries similar to:

```text
Published Name:     oem50.inf
Original Name:      ftdibus.inf
Provider Name:      FTDI
Class Name:         USB
Driver Version:     10/28/2024 2.12.36.20

Published Name:     oem52.inf
Original Name:      ftdiport.inf
Provider Name:      FTDI
Class Name:         Ports
Driver Version:     10/28/2024 2.12.36.20
```

The exact `oemXX.inf` numbers can be different on each PC.

## Verify the D2XX DLL

The installed FTDI D2XX library can be checked with:

```powershell
(Get-Item "$env:WINDIR\System32\ftd2xx.dll").VersionInfo |
    Select-Object FileVersion, ProductVersion
```

For driver package `2.12.36.20`, the result should look similar to:

```text
FileVersion ProductVersion
----------- --------------
3.2.21.1    2.12.36.20
```

## Remove Old FTDI Drivers

If old FTDI driver packages are still installed, they can be removed with `pnputil`.

First determine the correct `oemXX.inf` name:

```powershell
pnputil /enum-drivers | Select-String -Pattern "FTDI" -Context 5,5
```

Then remove the unwanted package, for example:

```powershell
pnputil /delete-driver oem143.inf
pnputil /delete-driver oem84.inf
```

The `oemXX.inf` numbers above are only examples. Use the numbers shown on your own system.

If Windows reports that administrator permissions are required, start PowerShell as **Administrator**.

# Building the Firmware

Open a command window in the firmware source folder.

## 64-bit Windows Build

For a clean 64-bit build:

```bash
make -f windows-Makefile clean
make -f windows-Makefile ARCH=x64
```

The default build can also be used if the Makefile defaults to x64:

```bash
make -f windows-Makefile clean
make -f windows-Makefile
```

## 32-bit Windows Build

For a clean 32-bit build:

```bash
make -f windows-Makefile clean
make -f windows-Makefile ARCH=x86
```

# Windows Build Environment

The firmware can be compiled using **MSYS2 / MinGW-w64**.

Download MSYS2 from:

https://www.msys2.org/

The default installation directory is:

```text
C:\msys64
```

# 32-bit Build Environment

## 1. Install MSYS2

Install MSYS2 using the default installation directory:

```text
C:\msys64
```

## 2. Start MSYS2

Open:

```text
MSYS2 MSYS
```

from the Windows Start menu.

## 3. Update MSYS2

Run:

```bash
pacman -Syu
```

If MSYS2 asks you to close the terminal, close it.

## 4. Finish the Update

Start **MSYS2 MSYS** again and run:

```bash
pacman -Syu
```

Repeat until all packages are up to date.

## 5. Install the 32-bit GCC Toolchain

Install the MinGW 32-bit GCC/G++ toolchain:

```bash
pacman -S --needed mingw-w64-i686-gcc
```

## 6. Add MinGW 32-bit to PATH

Open Windows PowerShell and run:

```powershell
$add = "C:\msys64\mingw32\bin"
$path = [Environment]::GetEnvironmentVariable("Path", "User")
$parts = $path -split ";" | Where-Object { $_ }

if ($parts -notcontains $add) {
    [Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable(
        "Path",
        (($parts + $add) -join ";"),
        "User"
    )
}
```

Close and reopen the terminal afterwards so the updated `PATH` is loaded.

## 7. Verify GCC

Check that GCC can be found:

```powershell
gcc --version
```

You can also check its location:

```powershell
where.exe gcc
```

For the 32-bit toolchain, the path should normally point to:

```text
C:\msys64\mingw32\bin\gcc.exe
```

## 8. Build

Go to the firmware folder and run:

```bash
make -f windows-Makefile clean
make -f windows-Makefile ARCH=x86
```

# 64-bit Build Environment

The setup for a 64-bit build is similar to the 32-bit environment.

## 1. Install the 64-bit GCC Toolchain

From MSYS2, install:

```bash
pacman -S --needed mingw-w64-x86_64-gcc
```

## 2. Add MinGW 64-bit to PATH

Open Windows PowerShell and run:

```powershell
$add = "C:\msys64\mingw64\bin"
$path = [Environment]::GetEnvironmentVariable("Path", "User")
$parts = $path -split ";" | Where-Object { $_ }

if ($parts -notcontains $add) {
    [Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable(
        "Path",
        (($parts + $add) -join ";"),
        "User"
    )
}
```

Close and reopen the terminal afterwards.

## 3. Verify GCC

Run:

```powershell
gcc --version
```

and:

```powershell
where.exe gcc
```

For the 64-bit toolchain, the path should normally point to:

```text
C:\msys64\mingw64\bin\gcc.exe
```

## 4. Build

Go to the firmware folder and run:

```bash
make -f windows-Makefile clean
make -f windows-Makefile ARCH=x64
```

or, if x64 is the default architecture:

```bash
make -f windows-Makefile clean
make -f windows-Makefile
```

# Switching Between 32-bit and 64-bit Builds

Make sure the correct MinGW directory occurs first in your Windows `PATH`.

For 32-bit:

```text
C:\msys64\mingw32\bin
```

For 64-bit:

```text
C:\msys64\mingw64\bin
```

You can check which compiler Windows currently uses with:

```powershell
where.exe gcc
```

After switching architectures, always perform a clean build:

```bash
make -f windows-Makefile clean
```

Then build the required version:

```bash
make -f windows-Makefile ARCH=x86
```

or:

```bash
make -f windows-Makefile ARCH=x64
```

